item.text:
Chapter 2B research wave completed for Vladimir Putin, Russian Federation, 2022 only. No score was assigned and no other chapter was researched.

The workspace was read-only and the active toolset provided no filesystem-read/write interface, so `research-ledger-manifest.json` could not be inspected or updated safely. The records below are an append-only merge payload; they must not replace the parent’s cumulative ledger.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"General Assembly Overwhelmingly Adopts Resolution Demanding Russian Federation Immediately End Illegal Use of Force in Ukraine, Withdraw All Troops","publisher":"United Nations","publication_date":"2022-03-02","url":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/ga12407.doc.htm","claim":"The General Assembly, by 141 votes to 5 with 35 abstentions, characterized Russia's operation as aggression violating the UN Charter, demanded withdrawal, and noted that peaceful mechanisms including Minsk, the Normandy Format, and the Trilateral Contact Group remained applicable.","locator":"Opening summary and paragraphs describing resolution ES-11/1, vote, withdrawal demand, and peaceful-resolution mechanisms","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-001","canonical_fact_key":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/ga12407.doc.htm|opening summary and ES-11/1 provisions|General Assembly characterized the invasion as aggression and identified peaceful alternatives","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.1","2B.2","2B.3","2B.7","2B.10"],"source_type":"intergovernmental resolution and meeting record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UN record with recorded vote and operative demands; individual states' characterizations are political, but the resolution outcome is directly verifiable.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly concerns decisions and available diplomatic channels immediately before and after 24 February 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Putin publicly ordered the military operation and held Supreme Commander-in-Chief authority; the resolution formally attributes the state conduct to the Russian Federation rather than deciding Putin's personal legal liability.","contrary_evidence":["Russia claimed collective self-defence of the separatist entities and protection of Donbas residents.","The resolution also called on all parties to comply with humanitarian law and work constructively through diplomatic frameworks."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.2","2B.3","2B.7","2B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"With 143 Votes in Favour, 5 Against, General Assembly Adopts Resolution Condemning Russian Federation’s Annexation of Four Eastern Ukraine Regions","publisher":"United Nations","publication_date":"2022-10-12","url":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/ga12458.doc.htm","claim":"The General Assembly condemned Russia's attempted annexation of four occupied Ukrainian regions, declared the actions inconsistent with the UN Charter, and demanded reversal and troop withdrawal.","locator":"Opening four paragraphs describing resolution ES-11/4 and the 143-5-35 recorded vote","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-002","canonical_fact_key":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/ga12458.doc.htm|opening four paragraphs|General Assembly condemned attempted annexation and demanded reversal and withdrawal","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.2","2B.7","2B.9","2B.10"],"source_type":"intergovernmental resolution and meeting record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UN account of a recorded General Assembly decision; it establishes the international institutional response, not individual criminal responsibility.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The annexation decisions and resolution occurred in September-October 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Putin announced and signed the accession measures; this source evaluates the Russian Federation's measures at state level.","contrary_evidence":["Russia asserted that the occupied populations exercised self-determination.","Thirty-five states abstained, although only five voted against the resolution."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.7","2B.9","2B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Secretary-General's remarks on Russian decision on annexation of Ukrainian territory","publisher":"United Nations Secretary-General","publication_date":"2022-09-29","url":"https://www.un.org/sg/en/content/sg/statements/2022-09-29/secretary-generals-remarks-russian-decision-annexation-of-ukrainian-territory-delivered","claim":"The Secretary-General said annexation following force would violate the UN Charter, that the occupation-period referendums could not represent genuine popular will, and that proceeding would escalate the conflict and further jeopardize peace.","locator":"Paragraphs beginning 'Any annexation of a State’s territory' through 'Any decision by Russia to go forward will further jeopardize the prospects for peace'","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-003","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.un.org/sg/en/content/sg/statements/2022-09-29/secretary-generals-remarks-russian-decision-annexation-of-ukrainian-territory-delivered|annexation legality and peace paragraphs|Secretary-General warned Putin-announced annexation would be unlawful and jeopardize peace","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.2","2B.7","2B.10"],"source_type":"senior intergovernmental official statement","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Authoritative statement of the UN Secretary-General's legal and institutional position; it overlaps the later General Assembly resolution and is therefore not independent corroboration of the same event.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Delivered one day before Putin's 30 September 2022 annexation ceremony.","ruler_attribution":"The statement responds directly to the Kremlin-announced process under Putin but does not adjudicate his individual legal liability.","contrary_evidence":["Russia described the votes as self-determination and accession rather than annexation.","The statement is an institutional legal assessment, not a judicial judgment."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.7","2B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Report on Killings of Civilians: Summary Executions and Attacks on Individual Civilians in Kyiv, Chernihiv, and Sumy Regions","publisher":"Office of the United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights","publication_date":"2022-12-07","url":"https://www.ohchr.org/sites/default/files/2022-12/2022-12-07-OHCHR-Thematic-Report-Killings-EN.pdf","claim":"OHCHR documented Russian forces' summary executions and attacks on individual civilians during their occupation of parts of northern Ukraine between 24 February and 6 April 2022.","locator":"Executive summary and findings; especially report page 17 discussion of Russian armoured columns and applicable prohibition on wilful killing","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-004","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.ohchr.org/sites/default/files/2022-12/2022-12-07-OHCHR-Thematic-Report-Killings-EN.pdf|executive summary and findings|Russian forces committed documented summary executions and attacks on civilians in northern Ukraine","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.9"],"source_type":"intergovernmental human-rights fact-finding report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Incident-based UN monitoring report applying corroboration standards; conflict access and geographic selection limit national prevalence inference.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Documents conduct from February-April 2022 and was published within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Strong attribution to Russian armed forces; Putin's command authority is established separately, but this report does not show his knowledge or personal order for each incident.","contrary_evidence":["The report addresses selected northern regions and does not measure all Russian units or operations.","Russian authorities disputed many international allegations and denied intentional attacks on civilians."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ukraine: ICRC calls for immediate and unimpeded access to Mariupol to facilitate safe passage of civilians and wounded","publisher":"International Committee of the Red Cross","publication_date":"2022-04-24","url":"https://www.icrc.org/en/document/ukraine-icrc-calls-immediate-and-unimpeded-access-mariupol-facilitate-safe-passage","claim":"The ICRC reported urgent unmet need for humanitarian access and voluntary safe passage for thousands of civilians and hundreds of wounded in Mariupol, while stating that workable arrangements required party agreement and security guarantees.","locator":"Lines 172-186, especially paragraphs at lines 177 and 183-186","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-005","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.icrc.org/en/document/ukraine-icrc-calls-immediate-and-unimpeded-access-mariupol-facilitate-safe-passage|lines 177-186|Mariupol required immediate humanitarian access and agreed security guarantees","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.7"],"source_type":"neutral humanitarian operational statement","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Neutral humanitarian organization reporting its own access efforts and operational requirements; it does not allocate exclusive responsibility for every failed arrangement.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Contemporaneous with the April 2022 siege of Mariupol.","ruler_attribution":"Shows a protection and access problem involving belligerent command structures; does not establish which individual blocked each proposed arrangement.","contrary_evidence":["The ICRC stated that practical agreements and security guarantees were required from the parties, not solely Russia.","Subsequent evacuations and the ICRC-facilitated passage of Azovstal combatants show that limited cooperation eventually occurred."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Olenivka penal facility: Prisoners of war and ICRC's role","publisher":"International Committee of the Red Cross","publication_date":"2022-08-03","url":"https://www.icrc.org/en/document/olenivka-penal-facility-prisoners-war-and-icrcs-role","claim":"The ICRC said it had not been allowed confidential individual access to POWs at Olenivka, despite repeated requests, while also recording that it facilitated combatants' safe passage from Azovstal and registered them as POWs.","locator":"Lines 202-215, particularly lines 207-208 and 212-215","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-006","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.icrc.org/en/document/olenivka-penal-facility-prisoners-war-and-icrcs-role|lines 202-215|ICRC lacked required Olenivka POW access but facilitated Azovstal safe passage","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.7"],"source_type":"neutral humanitarian operational statement","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"First-party account of ICRC access and facilitation; it explicitly distinguishes its role from a public war-crimes inquiry.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Addresses May-August 2022 detention and access events.","ruler_attribution":"The access failure concerns Russian-controlled or proxy-controlled detention structures, but the source does not establish a personal Putin instruction.","contrary_evidence":["The ICRC had limited physical access to the site in May and facilitated safe passage from Azovstal.","The ICRC expressly did not determine public responsibility for the Olenivka explosion."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Address by the President of the Russian Federation","publisher":"President of Russia","publication_date":"2022-09-21","url":"https://special.kremlin.ru/catalog/countries/UA/events/69390","claim":"Putin personally announced immediate partial mobilization, ordered regional cooperation and increased defence-industrial production, and justified escalation by describing Ukraine as a neo-Nazi regime and the conflict as liberation of Donbas.","locator":"Opening description of the conflict and paragraphs beginning 'Мобилизационные мероприятия начнутся сегодня' and 'Добавлю, что Указ о частичной мобилизации'","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-007","canonical_fact_key":"https://special.kremlin.ru/catalog/countries/UA/events/69390|mobilization and defence-order paragraphs|Putin ordered partial mobilization and defence-production expansion while repeating neo-Nazi framing","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.3","2B.6","2B.8","2B.9"],"source_type":"ruler speech and official executive announcement","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary evidence of Putin's own orders and stated rationale; claims about Ukrainian threats require independent verification and should not be accepted as fact merely because officially stated.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly dated 21 September 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct personal speech and order by Putin.","contrary_evidence":["Russia had suffered significant battlefield reversals before mobilization, providing a concrete military-manpower rationale in addition to ideological framing.","The speech promised training, military status, payments, and social guarantees for mobilized personnel."],"lenses":["2B.3","2B.6","2B.8","2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Trends in World Military Expenditure, 2022","publisher":"Stockholm International Peace Research Institute","publication_date":"2023-04-24","url":"https://www.sipri.org/sites/default/files/2023-04/2304_fs_milex_2022.pdf","claim":"SIPRI estimated Russian military spending rose 9.2 percent to $86.4 billion in 2022, while the national-defence allocation was revised 34 percent above its pre-war plan and mobilization and extra-force training spending more than doubled from 2021.","locator":"PDF page 3, lines 240-258","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-008","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.sipri.org/sites/default/files/2023-04/2304_fs_milex_2022.pdf|page 3 lines 240-258|Russian military and mobilization spending rose substantially during the invasion","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.8","2B.10"],"source_type":"independent quantitative research fact sheet","source_confidence":"medium-high","source_confidence_reason":"Established cross-national military-expenditure methodology, but SIPRI explicitly warns that Russian 2022 figures are highly uncertain because financial reporting became more opaque.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Measures full-year 2022 spending and revisions attributable mainly to the invasion.","ruler_attribution":"Putin ordered mobilization and defence-production expansion, but the figures describe state expenditure rather than tracing every allocation to his personal decision or to enrichment.","contrary_evidence":["Higher spending can reflect genuine wartime replacement and manpower requirements once the conflict was underway.","The source does not establish personal enrichment, patronage allocation, or the efficiency of each expenditure."],"lenses":["2B.8","2B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Security Council, 9143rd meeting: Maintenance of peace and security of Ukraine","publisher":"United Nations Security Council","publication_date":"2022-09-30","url":"https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/3989604/files/S_PV.9143-EN.pdf","claim":"Russia used its veto against a draft response to the occupied-region referendums and annexation; no other Council member recognized the annexation, while several members also urged renewed diplomacy and criticized the draft's ability to facilitate negotiations.","locator":"PDF pages 4-8, especially lines 475-535, 541-600, and 603-619","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-009","canonical_fact_key":"https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/3989604/files/S_PV.9143-EN.pdf|pages 4-8|Russia vetoed Security Council action concerning annexation amid broad non-recognition and some procedural dissent","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.2","2B.7","2B.9","2B.10"],"source_type":"intergovernmental verbatim meeting and voting record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official verbatim record and vote; member-state interpretations remain political positions and are retained with abstaining states' reservations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Same day as Putin's 30 September 2022 annexation announcement.","ruler_attribution":"The meeting repeatedly identifies Putin's announcement; the veto is formally an act of the Russian Federation.","contrary_evidence":["Brazil abstained and argued that the draft's scope and language did not favor an environment conducive to diplomacy.","China called for dialogue and a political settlement rather than supporting the proposed condemnation."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.7","2B.9","2B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The diplomatic efforts that led to the Trump-Putin meeting on Ukraine","publisher":"Associated Press","publication_date":"2025-08-13","url":"https://apnews.com/article/russia-ukraine-war-diplomatic-putin-trump-2a2c4bda9bb53ae2036837af899573a3","claim":"AP's diplomatic chronology records Russia-Ukraine negotiations in Istanbul in March 2022, Russia's stated willingness to reduce operations near Kyiv and Chernihiv, Lavrov's April rejection of a Ukrainian proposal as unacceptable, and the July grain agreement.","locator":"Chronology lines 1776-1779","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-010","canonical_fact_key":"https://apnews.com/article/russia-ukraine-war-diplomatic-putin-trump-2a2c4bda9bb53ae2036837af899573a3|lines 1776-1779|2022 talks produced movement but no settlement, followed by a successful limited grain agreement","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.1","2B.7"],"source_type":"retrospective news chronology","source_confidence":"medium-high","source_confidence_reason":"Reputable wire-service synthesis with links to underlying reports, but it is retrospective and does not resolve disputed reasons the negotiations failed.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"The reported negotiations and grain agreement occurred in 2022; publication is retrospective.","ruler_attribution":"Shows diplomacy conducted by the Russian state under Putin; it does not establish Putin's private instructions or which side caused the final breakdown.","contrary_evidence":["Ukraine showed openness to neutrality with external security guarantees.","Russia participated in negotiations and the later grain arrangement.","Draft terms, sequencing, Bucha revelations, battlefield developments, and external influence remain disputed causes of the talks' collapse."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7"]}

Research-accounting summary:

- Documents discovered: 54 plausible results across 16 broad, event-specific, institutional, adverse/contrary, and Russian-language queries.
- Documents inspected for plausibility: 39.
- Underlying pages/PDFs opened or directly attempted: 32.
- Successfully accessible underlying records: 12.
- Accepted new source-claim units: 10.
- Rejected: 15, principally duplicates, later-period commentary, unsupported opinion, Wikipedia/Reddit discovery leads, and sources that added no independent claim.
- Access-blocked or internally failed: 10, including the ICC situation page, Kremlin English pages, the ICJ page on explicit reopening, several UN pages, and OHCHR PDFs returning 403. Search-indexed contents were not treated as independent new documents where the underlying source could not be adequately localized.
- Reused and verified from the supplied index: 5 — E005/ICJ suspension order, E011/OHCHR Commission findings, E039/ICRC POW access, E040/UN grain initiative, and E047/Treasury recognition/pretext record.
- Not reused because not adequately reopened or verified this turn: E001, E012, E041, and E050.
- Distinct accepted organizations: 7 — UN General Assembly/Secretariat/Security Council, OHCHR, ICRC, Kremlin/President of Russia, SIPRI, Associated Press, and—among reused records—ICJ and U.S. Treasury.
- Source types: intergovernmental resolutions and legal records; human-rights fact-finding; neutral humanitarian operational records; ruler speech/executive action; quantitative research; and independent news chronology.

Lens coverage:

- 2B.1: UN record of available diplomatic mechanisms; Istanbul talks and limited Russian negotiating participation.
- 2B.2: invasion, recognition of separatist entities, expanded territorial claims, and attempted annexation.
- 2B.3: Putin’s defensive/NATO/Donbas justification contrasted with institutional findings of aggression and ideological “neo-Nazi” framing.
- 2B.4: documented killings and attacks on civilians, humanitarian-access failures, and POW-access restrictions.
- 2B.5: Russian-force and Russian/proxy-controlled detention evidence; ICJ order also addressed directed or supported irregular forces.
- 2B.6: Putin’s own threat narrative plus Treasury’s contrary finding that the genocide rationale was false.
- 2B.7: Minsk/Normandy/Contact Group alternatives, Istanbul talks, humanitarian arrangements, and the successful grain initiative; causes of broader talks’ collapse remain disputed.
- 2B.8: mobilization and defence-industrial expansion ordered by Putin, with SIPRI expenditure data.
- 2B.9: annexation despite international legal rejection, Russian veto use, and little documented public acceptance of responsibility; adversarial UK claims about deflection were retained only as contextual leads.
- 2B.10: invasion, annexation, regional military-spending response, and deterioration in the legal and security environment.

Residual gaps:

- No internal Russian command records establish Putin’s knowledge of, or instructions concerning, particular atrocities, POW facilities, proxy conduct, or targeting decisions.
- The research cannot resolve the contested counterfactual of whether the March-April negotiations could have produced a durable settlement, or which actor was decisive in their collapse.
- No independent audit connects 2022 military spending to personal enrichment or security-elite patronage.
- Public evidence does not establish the full chain of responsibility for every proxy or Wagner action.
- Russian internal after-action reviews, casualty accounting, disciplinary records, and deliberations about failed operational assumptions remain unavailable.
- Humanitarian and atrocity documentation is geographically and access selected; it is unsuitable as a national prevalence denominator.

Another Chapter 2B-specific wave would have limited marginal value unless it gained access to Russian command records, authenticated 2022 negotiation drafts and instructions, military procurement audits, or internal accountability proceedings. Additional open-web searching would mostly duplicate the mature UN, ICRC, OHCHR, Kremlin, SIPRI, and diplomatic record already represented here.
